<dialog class="guide-dialog" id="hosting-guide" aria-labelledby="guide-title"><div class="guide-top"><span class="eyebrow">A LITTLE GUIDANCE</span><button type="button" class="icon-button" id="close-guide" aria-label="Close hosting guide">×</button></div><h2 id="guide-title">You've got this.<br>Here's where to look.</h2><p class="guide-lead">Keep your hosting dashboard open alongside this wizard. These are the settings you may need.</p><div class="guide-tabs" role="group" aria-label="Hosting guide"><button type="button" data-guide="20i" aria-pressed="false">Multi8 Hosting / StackCP</button><button type="button" data-guide="cpanel" aria-pressed="false">cPanel</button><button type="button" data-guide="other" aria-pressed="true">Other hosting</button></div><div class="guide-host" data-guide-panel="20i" hidden><h3>In Multi8 Hosting / StackCP</h3><ol><li><strong>Choose Linux hosting.</strong> Open the hosting package for your domain in StackCP.</li><li><strong>Set the PHP version.</strong> Find “Change PHP Version” or PHP settings and select the version required by your project.</li><li><strong>Create a MySQL database.</strong> Find “MySQL Databases”. Copy the database name, user, password, and hostname exactly. Multi8 Hosting may use a remote database hostname.</li><li><strong>Set the public folder.</strong> In domain management, point your document root to the project's public folder, for example public_html/public when that matches your upload layout.</li><li><strong>Enable SSL.</strong> Activate SSL for the domain and wait for HTTPS to work before entering the final website address.</li></ol></div><div class="guide-host" data-guide-panel="cpanel" hidden><h3>In cPanel</h3><ol><li><strong>Set the PHP version.</strong> Use MultiPHP Manager or Select PHP Version. Your host may use a different label.</li><li><strong>Create a MySQL database.</strong> Use MySQL Database Wizard or MySQL Databases. Create a user, assign it to the database, and grant the required privileges.</li><li><strong>Include prefixes.</strong> Use the complete names shown in cPanel, such as account_website. Ask your host for the database hostname.</li><li><strong>Expose only public files.</strong> Under Domains, set the document root to the Laravel public folder. If the main domain's root is fixed, ask your developer to keep the application outside public_html and deploy only public files there.</li><li><strong>Check HTTPS.</strong> Use SSL/TLS Status or AutoSSL and confirm that your website opens with https://.</li></ol></div><div class="guide-host" data-guide-panel="other"><h3>In another hosting panel</h3><ol><li><strong>Choose a supported PHP version.</strong> Look for PHP settings or ask the hosting provider.</li><li><strong>Create a new MySQL database and user.</strong> Give the user access to the database. Copy the actual server hostname and full names.</li><li><strong>Set the public document root.</strong> Only the Laravel public folder should be available on the web. Ask your developer if the panel cannot change the root.</li><li><strong>Enable HTTPS.</strong> Install an SSL certificate for the final domain.</li><li><strong>Run the hosting check.</strong> The wizard shows what your project needs and how to resolve missing settings.</li></ol></div><div class="guide-common"><h3>What the statuses mean</h3><dl><div><dt><span class="status-dot pass" aria-hidden="true"></span>Passed</dt><dd>This check is satisfied.</dd></div><div><dt><span class="status-dot warn" aria-hidden="true"></span>Advice</dt><dd>Read the tip; you may need a follow-up task.</dd></div><div><dt><span class="status-dot fail" aria-hidden="true"></span>Needs attention</dt><dd>Resolve this issue, then run the check again.</dd></div></dl><details><summary>“Database connection failed”</summary><p>Check the database host, full database name, full username, and password in your panel. Make sure the user is assigned to the database. Your hosting account password will not normally work as the database password.</p></details><details><summary>A PHP extension or writable folder is missing</summary><p>Use the wizard's specific tip. Extensions may be selectable in your PHP panel or require host support. Your developer can check the ownership and permissions of storage and bootstrap/cache. Avoid making every file writable.</p></details><details><summary>The website shows 403, 404, or 500</summary><p>Check the document root, PHP version, uploaded vendor folder, built frontend assets, and the project's public/.htaccess file. Ask your developer to review the application logs; never share a configuration file or database password in a public support message.</p></details><details><summary>What needs to happen before I use this wizard?</summary><p>Your developer prepares a deployment containing Composer dependencies, built frontend files, the installer, and a private setup code. The browser wizard does not install Composer packages or build frontend assets on the server.</p></details></div><div class="guide-bottom"><p>Panel labels can vary by hosting provider.</p><button type="button" class="button button-primary" id="guide-done">Back to setup →</button></div></dialog>
